WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.862
Begin with the feature's actual needs.

2
00:00:02.862 --> 00:00:06.132
A profile image endpoint does not need the
same

3
00:00:06.132 --> 00:00:09.584
contract as a document repository or a CSV
import.

4
00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:13.399
Define the permitted file categories,
number of files, per file

5
00:00:13.399 --> 00:00:17.396
size, total request size, and relevant
dimensions or page counts.

6
00:00:17.396 --> 00:00:20.485
Decide whether archives or active content
are allowed.

7
00:00:20.485 --> 00:00:22.847
Specify retention, quotas, and replacement
behavior

8
00:00:22.847 --> 00:00:24.664
so storage consumption and existing

9
00:00:24.664 --> 00:00:27.026
attachments cannot change through
undocumented defaults.

10
00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.569
Authorize the operation against the target
resource

11
00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:32.294
and tenant separately from validating the
file.

12
00:00:32.294 --> 00:00:35.201
A well formed PDF can still be attached

13
00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.290
to a record the caller does not control.

14
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.561
Obtain identity from verified
authentication, resolve

15
00:00:40.561 --> 00:00:43.195
the intended resource, and check whether
its

16
00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:45.557
current state permits attachment or
replacement.

17
00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:47.737
Perform these checks before expensive
ingestion

18
00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:50.099
when the required context is available.

19
00:00:50.099 --> 00:00:52.279
Cookie authenticated routes also need
appropriate

20
00:00:52.279 --> 00:00:54.278
protection against cross site requests.

21
00:00:54.278 --> 00:00:57.367
Submitted ownership fields must not
override trusted context.

22
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.455
Configure limits at infrastructure and
multipart parsing layers.

23
00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:04.361
Count actual received bytes, files,
fields, and parts rather than

24
00:01:04.361 --> 00:01:07.813
trusting a size field or content length
declaration alone.

25
00:01:07.813 --> 00:01:11.629
Stop an excessive stream promptly and
clean up partial storage.

26
00:01:11.629 --> 00:01:15.535
A popular parser provides mechanisms, not
a complete upload policy.

27
00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:20.349
Its field filters, storage engine, error
paths, and limits all need review.

28
00:01:20.349 --> 00:01:24.074
Multipart fields can arrive after file
parts, so storage callbacks

29
00:01:24.074 --> 00:01:27.526
must not assume authorization metadata has
already been parsed.

30
00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:30.433
Buffering small files can be reasonable
when strict

31
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:33.521
size and concurrency limits bound
aggregate memory use.

32
00:01:33.521 --> 00:01:36.065
Larger uploads generally need bounded
streaming with

33
00:01:36.065 --> 00:01:38.790
backpressure or a controlled object
storage workflow.

34
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.060
Streaming still requires disk quotas,
deadlines, and cleanup.

35
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.058
For direct uploads, issue access to a
specific

36
00:01:45.058 --> 00:01:48.147
server chosen destination with limited
lifetime and scope.

37
00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:51.054
Verify the received object and required
checks before

38
00:01:51.054 --> 00:01:54.051
publication; a signed upload URL does not
establish

39
00:01:54.051 --> 00:01:57.140
that its eventual contents satisfy the
application contract.

